From: Felix Fietkau <nbd@nbd.name>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 20:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] AP MLD: let the driver count the AP Removal Timer

Depends on 602-ap-mld-announce-the-removal-of-an-affiliated-ap.patch,
603-ap-mld-remove-an-affiliated-ap-at-the-expiry.patch, 608 and 609,
and on the mac80211 patches that add
NL80211_EXT_FEATURE_AP_REMOVAL_TIMER_OFFLOAD.

Patch 602 counts the AP Removal Timer on the host: at each beacon
interval it sets the beacons of every link of the AP MLD with the next
count. On mt7996 each new template with the Critical Update flag starts
the critical update in the firmware again, so the flag stays set for the
whole countdown, past the DTIM Beacon that ends it (IEEE 802.11be-2024
35.3.10).

Where the driver declares NL80211_EXT_FEATURE_AP_REMOVAL_TIMER_OFFLOAD,
set the beacons once at the start of the removal and let the driver
count the timer. Until the expiry, no beacon of the AP MLD is set, as
the driver counts from the beacons it took at the start, like the
MediaTek SDK does. The host timer sets no beacon, and keeps the count of
the Probe Response frames and the times of the ucode link_remove method
current.

NL80211_CMD_LINKS_REMOVED on the AP interface reports the expiry, which
the driver sends after the Beacon frame with the count 1 and before the
removal TBTT, once it stopped the TX of the link. That event ends the
removal. At the event the other links drop the Reconfiguration
element, so that no frame at or after the removal TBTT carries it.
The stations get their frames and the link stops after the removal TBTT
(IEEE 802.11be-2024 35.3.6.3). hostapd sees no TBTT, but the first
Beacon frame with the element goes out less than one beacon interval
after the update of the removed link returns, so the removal TBTT comes
before count + 1 intervals after that return, and the stations get
their frames then. The times of the ucode link_remove method include
that interval.

The host count starts at the return of that update and stays at 1 until
the event, so the Probe Response frames carry the element until the
expiry, with a count never above that of the Beacon frames. If the event
is missing count + 2 beacon intervals after the return, hostapd logs it
and ends the removal as at the event, so that the beacons of the other
links are not held for ever. If the driver takes no beacon of the
removed link at the start, it does not count the timer, so the removal
fails and the beacons are set again without the element.

The driver times the expiry in whole seconds and counts one removal of
an AP MLD at a time. With the feature, a removal whose count of beacon
intervals is not a whole number of seconds is refused, and so is a
removal while another link of the AP MLD is being removed. Without the
feature, the count of 602 stays.

By IEEE 802.11be-2024 35.3.6.3, a non-AP MLD whose only setup link is
the removed one counts itself disassociated at the removal TBTT. At the
expiry event, which comes before that TBTT, clear the authorized flag
of each such station in hostapd and in the driver, so that mac80211
drops new data for it before the TBTT. The Deauthentication after the
TBTT of 603 stays. Without the offload, 603 clears the flag at the host
expiry, which also comes before that TBTT.

Add NL80211_EXT_FEATURE_SCS and NL80211_EXT_FEATURE_MSCS to
nl80211_copy.h as well, so that the feature numbers match the kernel
headers of the mac80211 patches.

Signed-off-by: Felix Fietkau <nbd@nbd.name>
